content.text:
Aight, so **Dependency Injection in C#** is like this:

Your class is a boss. It needs certain people to get work done — maybe a database guy, a logger guy, an email guy.

Now, a rookie boss does this:

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private EmailSender _emailSender = new EmailSender();

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        _emailSender.Send("Order placed!");
    }
}
```

That boss is hiring his own crew directly.

Problem is: now `OrderService` is tightly connected to `EmailSender`. If you wanna swap in a different email sender, test it, mock it, or use a fancy new notification system, you gotta crack open the class and mess with the code.

Bad business.

---

## Dependency Injection says:

“Don’t build your own crew. Have the crew delivered to you.”

Like this:

```csharp
public interface IEmailSender
{
    void Send(string message);
}

public class EmailSender : IEmailSender
{
    public void Send(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Sending email: {message}");
    }
}

public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IEmailSender _emailSender;

    public OrderService(IEmailSender emailSender)
    {
        _emailSender = emailSender;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        _emailSender.Send("Order placed!");
    }
}
```

Now `OrderService` ain’t makin’ its own `EmailSender`.

It just says:

> “I need someone who can send emails. I don’t care who. Bring me a guy who implements `IEmailSender`.”

That’s dependency injection.

---

## The container is the consigliere

In ASP.NET Core, you usually register your crew in `Program.cs`:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<OrderService>();
```

This tells the DI container:

> “Whenever somebody asks for `IEmailSender`, send `EmailSender`.”

Then when the app needs `OrderService`, the container sees the constructor:

```csharp
public OrderService(IEmailSender emailSender)
```

and goes:

> “No problem, boss. I know a guy.”

It creates `EmailSender`, hands it to `OrderService`, and everybody gets paid.

---

## Why use dependency injection?

Because it keeps your operation clean.

### Without DI:
Your classes are making their own dependencies.

```csharp
private EmailSender _emailSender = new EmailSender();
```

That’s tight coupling. Everybody knows too much. Nobody can disappear quietly.

### With DI:
Your class only depends on an interface.

```csharp
private readonly IEmailSender _emailSender;
```

Now you can swap implementations:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmailSender, SmsSender>();
```

Boom. Same `OrderService`, different messenger.

No drama.

---

## It helps with testing

Say you wanna test `OrderService`.

You don’t want it sending real emails like some lunatic.

So you pass in a fake:

```csharp
public class FakeEmailSender : IEmailSender
{
    public void Send(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Fake send: {message}");
    }
}
```

Then:

```csharp
var service = new OrderService(new FakeEmailSender());
service.PlaceOrder();
```

Now you can test the business logic without waking up the whole neighborhood.

---

## Common lifetimes in C#

When registering services, you choose how long the object lives.

```csharp
builder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
```

Here’s the street version:

### `Transient`
New guy every time.

```csharp
AddTransient<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
```

Every time someone asks, the container creates a fresh instance.

Good for lightweight, stateless services.

---

### `Scoped`
One guy per job.

```csharp
AddScoped<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
```

In a web app, usually one instance per HTTP request.

Everybody working on that same request gets the same instance.

Good for things like database contexts.

---

### `Singleton`
One guy for the whole empire.

```csharp
AddSingleton<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();
```

One instance for the entire app lifetime.

Good for shared, thread-safe, expensive-to-create services.

But be careful: if this guy keeps state and multiple people mess with him at once, things get ugly.

---

## Tiny ASP.NET Core example

```csharp
public interface IGreetingService
{
    string GetGreeting();
}

public class GreetingService : IGreetingService
{
    public string GetGreeting()
    {
        return "How you doin'?";
    }
}
```

Register it:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IGreetingService, GreetingService>();
```

Use it in a controller:

```csharp
public class HomeController : Controller
{
    private readonly IGreetingService _greetingService;

    public HomeController(IGreetingService greetingService)
    {
        _greetingService = greetingService;
    }

    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        return Content(_greetingService.GetGreeting());
    }
}
```

The controller doesn’t create `GreetingService`.

The container delivers it.

Like a package. No questions asked.

---

## The core idea

Dependency Injection means:

> A class should receive the objects it needs instead of creating them itself.

In gangster terms:

> Don’t go recruitin’ your own crew in every back alley. Tell the family what you need, and the family sends the right people.

That’s DI.

